## Why This Is Asked

This question tests building a scalable, privacy-preserving, multi-tenant ML platform with end-to-end governance, not just a single model. It emphasizes per-tenant isolation, drift monitoring, fairness constraints, and safe rollout strategies in production.

## Key Concepts

- Multi-tenant isolation and data governance
- Privacy-preserving techniques (DP/FL)
- Drift detection and alerting
- Fairness and per-tenant constraints
- Offline/online evaluation and canary rollouts
- Observability and rollback

## Code Example

```javascript
function driftScore(distA, distB) {
  // simplistic KL-divergence proxy
  let s = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < distA.length; i++) {
    const a = distA[i] + 1e-9, b = distB[i] + 1e-9;
    s += a * Math.log(a / b);
  }
  return s;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you design per-tenant feature schemas and a model registry?
- What latency/throughput targets and monitoring dashboards would you implement to catch regressions fast?

## Why This Is Asked
This checks practical CV setup, leakage awareness, and model selection on small data.

## Key Concepts
- Data leakage prevention
- Stratified cross-validation
- Pipeline preprocessing
- Model comparison metrics (AUROC, calibration)

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: stratified K-fold CV in JS-like syntax
const k = 5;
const folds = stratifiedKFoldSplit(labels, k);
for (const [trainIdx, testIdx] of folds) {
  const model = trainLogisticRegression(features[trainIdx], labels[trainIdx]);
  const preds = model.predictProba(features[testIdx]);
  console.log(rocAuc(labels[testIdx], preds));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle class imbalance if AUROC is similar across models?
- How could you incorporate feature engineering without leaking future information?

